    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d79">

                <pb facs="0270" n="230" sameAs="frus:frus1951v05/pg230"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">780.5/10–1851: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in Egypt</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Caffery</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Department of State </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">top secret</hi>
                    </seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Cairo</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1951-10-18T15:00:00+02:00">October 18, 1951—3
                            p. m.</date>
                    </dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>511. When note<anchor n="1" xml:id="d79-f1"/> was delivered (remytel 506, October
                        17)<anchor n="2" xml:id="d79-f2"/> Under Secretary Ministry Foreign Affairs,
                    who is ordinarily a reasoned individual, spent half hour tearing MEC proposals
                    apart.</p>
                <p>I must remind Dept that our Ambassadors expected this type of reaction, and it
                    was for this reason that we so earnestly recommended that proposals not be put
                    forward at this time (mytel 447, October 10).<anchor n="3" xml:id="d79-f3"/>
                </p>
                <p>Having torn them to pieces, we cannot hope that Egyptians will give any further
                    consideration to proposals as they now stand. If we are to take advantage of
                    crack in door remytel 506 we will have to come up with proposals which at least
                    will have to be reworded. We must realize that Egyptians can only think of the
                    problem in <pb facs="0271" n="231" xml:id="pg231"/> terms of past history, and
                    cannot see the realities of the present World situation. We must not expect them
                    to consider the problem logically. Sent Dept 511, rptd info London 209, Paris
                    89, Ankara 71.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Caffery</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
